<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Einzel.Shell</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>einzel-shell</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      net10.0 rather than net10.0-windows, which is the whole point: this shell runs
      where the engine runs. OutputType WinExe suppresses the console window on Windows
      and is ignored elsewhere.
    -->

    <!--
      Pointers, for one reason: uploading a vertex buffer and setting a matrix uniform
      hand OpenGL a block of memory, and there is no safe spelling of that. Confined to
      the shell, which owns no physics - the engine's own inner loop is safe code that
      allocates nothing, which is a different discipline and stays that way.
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>

    <!--
      The same reversal the WPF shell needs, for a different reason and with the same
      argument behind it. Text layout and number entry in a window are locale-facing;
      what invariant globalization was protecting is that CLI output and golden files do
      not move with the host locale (CLI-5), and that is achieved by passing
      CultureInfo.InvariantCulture explicitly at every formatting and parsing site
      rather than by the build flag. A value typed into the model tree is parsed
      invariantly whatever the locale, because the file being edited is invariant.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>

    <Description>
      The shell, cross-platform. Spec section 16's eleven views on Avalonia, replacing
      the Windows-only WPF presentation layer. UI-1 is the constraint that shapes it:
      the shell owns layout, input, the interactive viewport and the update check, and
      owns no physics, no validation rules, no file format knowledge and no render
      output. Amendment 25 adds the constructive half - every action here must be
      expressible as a CLI invocation and journalled as one, so a capability with no
      command spelling cannot be added to the window, and a person's session hands over
      to an agent in the same vocabulary.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The only reference, and the boundary UI-1 is about. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Einzel.Commands\Einzel.Commands.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" />
    <PackageReference Include="Silk.NET.OpenGL" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
